<p>This brief covers stories from 23 to 26 November.</p>

<figure class="dgm">
<ol class="dgm-flow g3">
<li><b>List</b><span>The customer asks the agent for items</span></li>
<li><b>Match</b><span>The agent passes the list; the retailer matches products</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Sign in</b><span>First time, the customer signs in to the retailer and returns to the agent</span></li>
<li class="hl"><b>Mandate</b><span>An agent profile sets what the agent may do, with a spending cap</span></li>
<li><b>Pay</b><span>Checkout uses a stored card; the agent never sees card details</span></li>
<li><b>Fulfil</b><span>The retailer owns the order, returns and support</span></li>
</ol>
<figcaption>How an agent checkout can work without handing an agent your card: the pattern behind Walmart's gateway, as reported.</figcaption>
</figure>

<section class="block">
<h2>1. Walmart opens its Agent Gateway to Meta's Muse</h2>
<p>Walmart let Meta's Muse agent shop and complete checkout through its Agent Gateway: Muse passes a shopping list, Walmart matches products, and checkout happens in the Muse conversation using a card saved in the customer's Walmart account<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Customers sign in to Walmart the first time and can create an agent profile with a spending cap, so Muse completes authorised purchases without receiving card details; Walmart describes the gateway as a beta it expects to open to more agents<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> this is a concrete template for retailers deciding between blocking agents and letting them scrape: a dedicated agent channel where the retailer keeps identity, product matching, payment and the customer relationship, and the agent gets a scoped mandate. If you run e-commerce, design your own version before agents arrive unannounced: an agent API, customer-linked agent profiles with limits, and clear liability for returns and errors. UK readers should also note the FCA's position on <a href="/insights/fca-agentic-ai-architecture-money-moving-agents/">agents that move money</a>; see also <a href="/insights/ai-agents-can-pay-agentic-commerce/">agentic commerce</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>2. Anthropic launches Claude Ship for pull requests</h2>
<p>Anthropic launched Claude Ship, a set of agents for GitHub pull requests: Code Review, generally available, plus PR Steward, which fixes failing checks and merge conflicts, and Claude Approvals, which can approve routine changes when a policy file in the repository allows it; the latter two are in beta<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Coverage notes Anthropic's own warning that the service may miss bugs or security issues, and reports a recommendation to start approvals in observation mode<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> automated review is low risk and worth trialling; automated approval is a change to your control environment. If you try it, keep approvals off for anything touching authentication, payments, infrastructure or data handling, version the policy file like code with its own reviewers, and run observation mode long enough to compare the agent's decisions with your engineers'. Auditors will ask who approved a change; make sure the answer is recorded. See <a href="/insights/ai-coding-policy-engineering-teams/">AI coding policy for engineering teams</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>3. Microsoft powers up its first AMD Helios rack</h2>
<p>AMD said Microsoft has powered up its first Helios rack-scale AI system in a research lab and is running early workloads for validation, with Azure planning to deploy Helios at scale for frontier models and customer applications<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. No deployment size has been disclosed.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> a second rack-scale supplier in a major cloud is good news for price and availability over time, but it will reach customers as new instance types first. When they appear, test your own models on them before committing; serving software, kernels and memory behaviour differ between vendors. Keeping your serving stack portable makes that a benchmark, not a migration. See the <a href="/case-studies/brazil-ai-saas-accelerator-portable-inference/">accelerator-portable inference case study</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>4. South Korea drafts rules for using personal data in AI development</h2>
<p>South Korea's Personal Information Protection Commission published draft amendments to the enforcement decree of the Personal Information Protection Act, setting conditions for a new exception that allows personal data to be used for AI development where anonymised or pseudonymised data will not do, with stronger security and prior privacy risk assessments for higher-risk uses<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. The IAPP has described the approach as relying heavily on regulator review rather than detailed rules<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>.</p>
<p><strong>CTO take:</strong> for companies training or fine-tuning models in Korea, this offers a lawful route, but only with evidence: why de-identified data is insufficient, what safeguards apply, and a risk assessment. Build that documentation into your training pipeline now, recording datasets, purposes and access, so an application is a report rather than a project. See the <a href="/case-studies/korea-ai-basic-act-hiring-platform/">Korea AI Basic Act case study</a> and <a href="/insights/model-provenance-open-weight-models/">model provenance</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do this week</h2>
<ul>
<li>Retail and payments teams: sketch an agent channel with customer-linked profiles and limits.</li>
<li>Engineering leaders: trial AI code review; keep automated approval in observation mode.</li>
<li>Platform teams: plan to benchmark new accelerator instance types on your own models.</li>
<li>Teams training on Korean data: start recording datasets, purposes and safeguards per training run.</li>
</ul>
<p>I am a <a href="/fractional-cto/">fractional CTO</a> and <a href="/ai-architect/">AI architect</a> based in India, the United States and the UAE, working globally. If any of these lands on your desk this week, <a href="/#assess">analyse your challenge</a> and bring the result to a call.</p>
</section>
